WEBVTT

00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:06.477
A robot is crossing a building.

00:00:06.537 --> 00:00:10.311
It already has a plan. And the plan was
not cheap.

00:00:11.862 --> 00:00:17.602
To find this route, the planner examined a
hundred and twenty five thousand cells.

00:00:17.662 --> 00:00:21.526
Nine hundred and nineteen of them became
the path.

00:00:23.676 --> 00:00:26.462
Then something is left in the way.

00:00:26.521 --> 00:00:31.721
The plan is now wrong. The robot needs a
new one, and it needs it now.

00:00:36.271 --> 00:00:39.338
The standard answer is to start again.

00:00:39.398 --> 00:00:44.804
A* throws away everything it knew, and
searches the building from scratch.

00:00:47.354 --> 00:00:53.529
D* Lite keeps its previous search, and
repairs only what the obstacle changed.

00:00:53.589 --> 00:00:56.947
Here, that meant two hundred and fifty six
cells.

00:00:57.007 --> 00:01:01.597
Same goal. Same optimal cost. A fraction
of the work.

00:01:06.747 --> 00:01:11.994
The benchmark repeats that moment fourteen
hundred and ninety three times.

00:01:12.054 --> 00:01:15.102
Two hundred seeded journeys through this
map,

00:01:15.052 --> 00:01:18.950
with an obstacle dropped on the path,
again and again.

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:26.132
On average, D* Lite replans four times
faster. On the median event, eleven times.

00:01:27.882 --> 00:01:30.110
Every event is a point.

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:35.450
From scratch, the cost swings with how
much of the building must be searched
again.

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:38.338
Repair stays in a narrow band.

00:01:38.398 --> 00:01:44.166
And both planners returned paths of
exactly equal cost, on every measurement.

00:01:48.917 --> 00:01:51.155
The repair is not free.

00:01:51.215 --> 00:01:55.683
D* Lite's first plan costs almost three
times as much.

00:01:55.743 --> 00:01:58.993
And on a third of the events, the trivial
ones,

00:01:58.943 --> 00:02:03.123
it loses. Its bookkeeping costs more than
a short search.

00:02:04.873 --> 00:02:07.740
Correctness is checked, not assumed.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:09.678
Costs are exact integers,

00:02:09.628 --> 00:02:15.339
because floating point ties once froze
stale search state into the answer.

00:02:15.399 --> 00:02:21.216
A fuzzer then checked a hundred and eighty
five thousand replans against Dijkstra.

00:02:21.166 --> 00:02:22.451
Zero failures.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:31.632
The timings come from one machine, and
will vary with hardware.

00:02:31.692 --> 00:02:35.025
The path in this film is real planner
output.

00:02:34.975 --> 00:02:38.704
The robot's motion along it is an
illustration.

00:02:39.655 --> 00:02:42.247
ROS2 Dynamic Path Planning.

00:02:42.306 --> 00:02:45.609
Incremental replanning, measured honestly.
